HST Office: popup and context menus get the frame's coat: a rounded outline, the shortcut colour and the GTK menu surface.

- Popup menus that VCL draws itself (Windows): with the frame's switch for menus on
  (org.openoffice.Office.HSTAppearance/FrameCoat/Menus, under UiCoat/Enabled and HST_UI_COAT), the menu's surface
  gets a thin outline in the menu border colour, rounded with radius::Menu at the edge of the content. The window
  keeps square corners; the ring of its border window, whose width VCL keeps, takes the surface colour, so the
  rounded outline is the only one. A shortcut is drawn in the shortcut colour where it reads (4.5:1) on the surface
  or the highlight; a disabled item keeps VCL's disabled colour.
- Nothing changes a size: item heights, the menu's border widths, the check/icon column and the text and shortcut
  positions stay VCL's. The menus' order and commands are unchanged. High contrast, the coat switched off and
  FrameCoat/Menus=false keep the previous look.
- Thin hooks: MenuFloatingWindow::ApplySettings colours the border window's ring; Menu::ImplPaint asks for the
  shortcut colour in its shortcut block only (the VCL-drawn path; the native menus are not touched).
- GTK 3 (native menus): the menu surface, its outline colour, radius::Menu, the item text, the scroll arrows and
  the popup window's background, plus the decoration's corners and outline ring under a compositor (Adwaita's
  shadow geometry, so no window changes its size). Colours and corner radii only.
- The logic is in vcl/source/hst/coat/coat-menus.cxx (<hst/coat-menus.hxx>) and vcl/source/hst/coat/css/menus.cxx;
  CppunitTest_vcl_hst_menu_coat tests it.

